NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

This example shows the CAN interpreter displaying CAN data transmitted by a Microchip
MCP2515. It also shows two instances of the SPI interpreter displaying SPI MISO and 
MOSI data which is being sent/received by the circuit's microcontroller.

A cursor has been place next to each of several points of interest in this captured data. 
From within the Waveforms or State List tabs, you can quickly scroll to cursors A through F 
by pressing the <A> through <F> keys on your keyboard. 

By zooming in near each of the cursors, the following sequence can be observed:

At cursor A, the microcontroller begins setting up the MCP2515. You can see the 
microcontroller sending SPI data to setup the MCP2515's control registers.

     You can quickly zoom in or out by pressing the <+> or <-> keys on the numerical 
     keypad of your keyboard. 

At cursor B, the microcontroller begins sending the SPI frames which contain the 
actual data to be transmitted on the CAN bus. The first data byte value is 01h.
Seven more bytes of data are then sent to the MCP2515's buffer in preparation 
for the CAN transmission.

At cursor C, the microcontroller sends the final control data thereby initiating a CAN
transmission. You can see the transmission begin at the trigger position.

     You can press T on your keyboard to quickly scroll to the trigger position.

At cursor D, The microcontroller begins polling the MCP2515 to see if the frame
has been completed. A value of 0Bh is being returned, indicating the frame 
has not yet finished.

At cursor E, you can see the first byte of data being transmitted over the CAN bus.
It has a value of 01h, and is followed by the data bytes 23h, 45h, 67h, 89h, ABh,
CDh, and finally EFh. These are the actual data payload for the CAN frame. All other 
fields of the frame are overhead for the CAN protocol.

At cursor F, the value 03h is finally returned to the microcontroller, indicating that 
the frame is complete. The controller stops polling at this point, and there is no further
activity on any of the signals.

     You can right click on the "Signal" cell of the waveform table and select 
     "Expand All" from the drop down menu to quickly expand all of the interpreters.
     This exposes the individual signals allowing you to observe the original raw data.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain intact
for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning purposes, 
but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the "Save As" option to 
save an editable copy of any example file.
/
